9. ITEM 2 -- a first attack on C1 for k >= 3, and the counting fact it rests on
==============================================================================
   C1-C asks for ONE realization with alpha <= k.  alpha(G) <= k follows from a
   cover of V(G) by k cliques.  M has (w+1) + k vertices; assign s_i of the w+1
   pad vertices to part i and make {q_i} u S_i a clique.  That needs s_i <= lam_i
   with sum s_i = w+1, i.e. it needs sum(lam) >= lam_1 + 1.
   'sum(lam) >= lam_1 + 1 for every lam with k >= 2' : 28598 partitions, 0 failures
   (immediate: a second part is >= 1.  So the clique-cover assignment is never
    blocked by the counting condition -- what is unproved is that a realization
    of M CONTAINING those k cliques exists.)

   A PROVED special case of the same idea, checked here: if lam is itself
   graphical then K_{w+1} (disjoint) H realizes M and alpha = 1 + alpha(H) <= k,
   because H has an edge.  Verified by exhaustive alpha over small realizations:
   graphical lam with |M| <= 11 : 60 cases, C1 violations : 0

